NOTES = {
"title": "This chapter is about threads: how a program starts work that runs beside the main program, how it waits for that work, and what goes wrong when threads share values. It takes the multithreading section of chapter 17 of the 2nd edition of the book, which the 3rd edition dropped, and runs it with Python 3.14.6. It has seventeen examples that run on the chapter page in the order of this deck. The downloader asks a local stand-in and never the real xkcd.com, and the timings of slide 38 were recorded once on a machine with 2 CPUs.",
"route": "The chapter has five parts. Part 1 starts, joins and ends threads and passes arguments to them. Part 2 runs the book's downloader as printed and with start() added. Part 3 shows lost updates and the tools against them: locks, events, semaphores and queues. Part 4 uses pools of threads and their Future objects. Part 5 compares the global interpreter lock, processes and asyncio, and slides 38 and 39 hold the recorded timings and five interpreters. See slide 3 for the two questions that the parts answer.",
"questions": "Two questions frame the chapter. The first asks what a program needs to start, wait for and end threads, and where the book's downloader goes wrong; parts 1 and 2 answer it, and the summary on slide 41 repeats it. The second asks when threads lose updates or wait for ever and which tool fits which work; parts 3 to 5 answer it, and the table on slide 38 and the questions on slide 40 close it. Also see slide 2 for the route through the parts.",
"divider1": "Part 1 starts with the thread itself. The first example starts a function on a second thread and shows that its line comes last. The second passes arguments with args and kwargs and shows what happens when a call stands in place of the target. The third shows that a thread can be started once and that its error stays in it. The fourth waits with join(), and the fifth marks a daemon thread. Slide 5 lists the concepts first; see also slide 12 for the project that uses them.",
"divider2": "Part 2 is the book's project, a downloader that gets comics with one thread for each range of ten numbers. The first example runs the listing as printed, which never starts its threads. The second adds start() and counts which comic numbers were asked for. A local stand-in answers the requests on this machine, so the real site is never contacted. Compare slide 7, where one thread was started, with slide 16, where fourteen were. Slide 13 lists the concepts of the project.",
"divider3": "Part 3 is about threads that share values. The first example shows two threads that both read 0 and both write 1, and then the same code with a lock. The second example shows the with statement and an RLock. The third shows two locks taken in opposite orders, and the fourth limits how many threads are inside with a semaphore. The fifth hands items over with a queue and wakes a thread with an Event. Slide 18 lists the tools; see slide 38 for the recorded race.",
"divider4": "Part 4 hands the starting and reuse of threads to a pool. The first example compares map(), which keeps the order of the inputs, with as_completed(), which gives the results as they finish, and counts the threads a pool uses by default. The second shows that an error in a thread comes out of result() and that tasks that have not started can be cancelled. Slide 27 lists the concepts; compare slide 7, where one thread was started by hand.",
"divider5": "Part 5 asks which tool fits which work. The first example asks two builds of Python 3.14.6 whether the global interpreter lock is on. The second shows that a process changes only its own copy of a variable and starts a program with Popen. The third shows asyncio overlapping waits on one thread and a blocking call stalling it. Slides 38 and 39 hold what the research recorded. Slides 32 and 33 list the concepts, and see slide 24 for the lock of a semaphore.",
"A1": "These ten rows are the first calls of the chapter. threading is the module, and threading.Thread() makes a thread object whose target is the function it runs. start() begins the thread and can be called once, join() waits until it has ended, and time.sleep() waits without doing anything. current_thread() returns the thread that runs the line. threadDemo.py is the book's first program, and slide 7 runs it. See slide 6 for the arguments and daemons, and slide 3 for the questions.",
"A1b": "Four more concepts finish the thread's first calls. The target receives its arguments from args, a list of positional arguments, and from kwargs, a dictionary of keyword arguments. A daemon thread does not keep the program alive: when the main thread ends, the daemon thread is cut off, and the flag must be set before start(). Slide 8 shows args and kwargs, slide 11 shows the daemon, and slide 9 shows what a thread does with an error. Compare slide 5 for the first calls.",
"A2": "The book's project downloads comics with threads. Step 1 turns the download loop into a function, downloadXkcd(), that takes a start and an end number. Step 2 makes one thread for each range and starts it. Step 3 calls join() for every thread, so that the program ends only after all are done. The file threadedDownloadXkcd.py is the finished project. Slide 15 runs the listing as printed and slide 16 runs it with start() added; see slide 14 for the story of xkcd.",
"A3": "These seven concepts are the tools for shared data. Concurrency issues arise when two threads change the same value and their steps interleave. A Lock is held by one thread at a time, and an RLock can be taken again by the thread that holds it. An Event is a flag that waiting threads watch, and a Semaphore is a counter that lets a fixed number of threads in. queue is the module and Queue the first-in first-out queue that hands items over. Slides 20 to 25 run them.",
"A4": "A pool starts threads, reuses them and returns each result in a Future. ThreadPoolExecutor() makes the pool, submit() starts one call and returns a Future at once, and map() calls a function for every input and returns the results in the order of the inputs. future.result() waits for the call and returns its value or raises its error. max_workers is the number of threads; the default is the number of CPUs plus 4, at most 32. See slides 29 and 30.",
"A5": "The global interpreter lock lets one thread at a time run Python code in the standard build. The free-threaded build of Python 3.13 and later can run without it, sys._is_gil_enabled() tells whether it is on, and PYTHON_GIL=1 switches it on again. Python 3.14 also has sub-interpreters, with the module concurrent.interpreters and the pool InterpreterPoolExecutor. Slide 35 asks two builds, slide 39 five interpreters, and slide 38 holds the recorded speed. See slide 34 for the story of two cores.",
"A5b": "Processes and asyncio are the other tools of the part. multiprocessing runs a function in another process with its own copy of the variables, and ProcessPoolExecutor is a pool of such processes. subprocess starts other programs, and Popen() returns at once, with poll() asking and wait() waiting. asyncio runs tasks on one thread: gather() runs awaitable calls together and to_thread() hands a blocking call to a thread. The summary and the practice questions close the chapter. See slides 36 and 37.",
"start": "The first program prints Start of program, then End of program, and only then Wake up, because the new thread sleeps for half a second while the main thread goes on. join() makes the main thread wait for it. In the second box the target saw the name Thread-2 (f) and 2 active threads, and the main thread is called MainThread. The first thread of the process was Thread-1, so the numbers in names count the threads made. Compare slide 10 for join and see slide 8.",
"args": "The list args became the three positional arguments of print(), and the dictionary kwargs became its keyword argument sep, so the first call printed Cats & Dogs & Frogs and the second, with the separator '& ', printed Cats& Dogs& Frogs. The last call is a mistake that looks right: print('Now', 'not later') ran at once in the main thread and returned None, so the thread had no target and nothing to run. Compare slide 7, where the target was given without brackets.",
"life": "A thread object has a name of its own: the first is called Thread- followed by a number, and a name can be given. start() can be called once, and a second call raised RuntimeError with the text threads can only be started once. An error in a thread does not stop the main thread: threading.excepthook received the ValueError, and the main thread printed main continues. Without a hook Python prints the traceback of the thread. See slide 30, where a pool keeps the error in a Future.",
"join": "join(0.1) waited at most 0.1 seconds and returned None, and the thread was still alive; the join of the second call waited until the sleep had ended, and then the thread was no longer alive. So the return value of join() does not say whether the thread ended; is_alive() does. A thread that was never started cannot be joined: join() raised RuntimeError with the text cannot join thread before it is started. See slide 15, where the printed downloader fails in this way.",
"daemon": "Two child programs started a thread that sleeps for half a second and then prints late. With daemon=True the program printed only end, because the daemon thread was cut off when the main thread ended; with daemon=False it printed end and late, because the program waited for the thread. The flag cannot be changed afterwards: setting daemon on a thread that is running raised RuntimeError. Compare slide 10, where join() waited for a thread, and slide 22, where daemon threads stay stuck.",
"printed": "This is the listing of the book's project as printed: it makes fourteen thread objects, one for each range of ten comics, and puts them in a list, but it never calls start(). So the stand-in was asked for no comic page, which printed 0, and the loop of join() calls raised RuntimeError at the first thread, with the text cannot join thread before it is started. Compare slide 10 and see slide 16, where start() is added.",
"fixed": "With start() the fourteen threads ran and the program asked the stand-in for 125 of the 139 comic numbers. The ranges are range(start, end) with end = i + 9, so the end number is never asked for: 9, 19, 29 and every tenth number after them, fourteen numbers up to 139, were never requested. The picture draws the numbers 1 to 139, green for asked and orange for never asked. The listing of the book has this difference; see slide 15 for the printed version and slide 14 for the story.",
"lost": "Both threads read the counter before either wrote it back, so each wrote 0 plus 1, and the counter ended at 1 in all three trials without a lock. The sleep of 0.05 seconds between the read and the write makes this certain; without it the same mistake is possible but rare. With the lock around the read and the write the counter ended at 2 in all three trials. See slide 21 for the with statement, slide 38 for a race that was recorded without a sleep, and slide 19 for a story.",
"lock": "Four threads each added 1 a hundred thousand times with the with statement, and the total was 400000. The with statement releases the lock also after an error, so locked() printed False after the ValueError. A Lock taken a second time by the thread that holds it waits, and the timeout of 0.2 seconds made acquire() return False; an RLock can be taken again by the same thread, and printed True. See slide 20 for the same counter without a lock and slide 22 for two locks.",
"dead": "Each thread took one lock, waited 0.1 seconds, and then asked for the lock that the other thread held, so both waited for ever. After 0.5 seconds both threads were still alive, which printed True twice. The threads are daemon threads, so the program could still end. The usual remedy is to take the locks in the same order in every thread. Compare slide 21, where one lock was used, and slide 25, where a thread waits on an Event, which another thread sets.",
"sem": "Six threads each stayed 0.1 seconds in the guarded block. With a BoundedSemaphore of 2 the most that were inside at once was 2, and with a limit of 6 it was 6. A semaphore is a counter that lets a fixed number of threads in, so it limits work such as downloads that a site may be asked for only a few at a time. BoundedSemaphore raises an error if it is released too often. See slide 23 for the story of Dijkstra and slide 21 for the lock.",
"queue": "The main thread put the numbers 0 to 4 and then None in a queue, and the consumer thread took them in the order they were put and stopped at None, so it received [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]. get() waits until an item is there. In the second box the thread waited on an Event: before set() the list was empty, and after set() it held woke. A queue and an Event let threads hand over values and signals without sharing a variable. Compare slide 20 and see slide 29.",
"pool": "map() returned [0, 1, 2], the order of the inputs, although the call for 2 finished first; as_completed() gave [2, 1, 0], the order in which the calls finished. A pool with two workers named its threads dl_0 and dl_1 and reused them for four calls. With no max_workers the pool used 6 threads for 40 calls on a machine with 2 CPUs, which is the number of CPUs plus 4. See slide 27 for the concepts and slide 30 for the Future objects.",
"future": "The error of the call stayed in the Future: exception() returned the ValueError and result() raised it in the main thread. result(timeout=0.1) raised TimeoutError while the call was still running, and done() was False. In the second box shutdown(cancel_futures=True) cancelled 4 of 5 tasks, because the one worker had already started the first. So a pool does not lose the error of a thread, unlike the case of slide 9, and cancelled tasks never run.",
"gil": "The standard build printed True for the lock and 0 for Py_GIL_DISABLED. The free-threaded build of Python 3.14.6 printed False, so its lock is off, and with the environment variable PYTHON_GIL=1 it printed True again. So one build can run with or without the lock. The recorded effect is on slide 38: only the free-threaded build lost updates in the race and ran work on the CPU faster on four threads. Slide 39 asks five interpreters the same kind of question.",
"proc": "A child process received a copy of the dictionary and changed its copy, so the dictionary of the parent still printed {'n': 0}; a thread changed the same object, and it printed {'n': 99}. The default start method printed here is forkserver. Popen() came back at once, and poll() returned None while the child still slept; wait() returned the return code 0. Processes avoid the lock but cost more to start, which slide 38 does not time; see slide 37 for the other tool.",
"async": "Ten awaits of 0.3 seconds took under 0.6 seconds, so the waits overlapped on one thread. Five blocking calls of 0.2 seconds took over a second, because a blocking call stalls every task on the thread. The same five calls handed to threads with to_thread() took under 0.6 seconds, and their threads were named asyncio_0 and so on. So asyncio fits many waits, and to_thread() keeps the loop free of a blocking call. Compare slide 35 and see slide 38 for the recorded waiting times.",
"compare_record": "The chart is the recorded time of four threads divided by the time of four tasks one after another, on 2 CPUs; below 1 means faster. Only the free-threaded 3.14.6 was clearly faster, at 0.40; 3.11.15 was at 0.94, and the other builds were slower, at 1.13 to 1.19. The table counts the updates lost of 4,000,000 in three runs of four threads that add 1 a million times without a lock: only the free-threaded build lost any, between 2.5 and 2.9 million. These were measured once on 2026-10-14. See slides 20 and 35.",
"compare_envs": "The same probe ran under five interpreters in this deck's own run. Python 3.11 and 3.12 have no call that tells about the lock, so the cell says no such call. From 3.13 the call exists, and the free-threaded 3.14.6 printed False for the lock and 1 for Py_GIL_DISABLED. The default start method of processes is forkserver from 3.14, and InterpreterPoolExecutor exists only in 3.14. See slides 35 and 33 for what these names mean.",
"practice": "These are the practice questions, and each points to the part where the idea was shown. The first is about join() without start() and the order of the lines, slides 7, 10 and 15. The second is about the lost update and the RLock, slides 20 and 21. The third is about map(), as_completed() and result(), slides 29 and 30. The fourth is about the recorded race and the blocking call, slides 37 and 38. The fifth chooses a tool, slides 38 and 39.",
"summary": "Four claims carry the chapter. A thread runs only after start(), and join() waits for it; the printed downloader never starts its threads. A shared counter needs a lock, and a semaphore, an event and a queue order the threads. A pool starts and reuses threads and returns results and errors in Future objects. Threads overlap waiting, only the free-threaded build sped up work on the CPU, and processes and asyncio fit other work. See slide 3 for the two questions.",
"resources": "These thirteen links are where to go next. They include the chapter of the book in its 2nd edition and the 3rd edition's page, the documentation of threading, concurrent.futures and asyncio, the Python guide to free threading and PEP 703, and the pages behind the five stories. See slide 3 for the questions that the chapter answers. On 2026-11-27 all thirteen pages answered; the page of the book's 2nd edition answered at the second ask, after a request limit at the first.",
}

